module bindings.javascript.birpc_cdp_proxy_without_permission_gate;

// birpc documents `createBirpc(functions, options)` as the half of a
// bidirectional RPC where every property of `functions` becomes a method the
// other side of the transport can call by name. When the transport is the
// WebSocket a dev server or test runner exposes, the peer is whatever page or
// client connected, so each registered handler is a network-reachable entry
// point that runs with the server's own privileges.
//
// A raw Chrome DevTools Protocol session is the highest privilege such a peer
// can be handed. The session-acquisition calls are documented library APIs --
// Playwright's `context.newCDPSession(page)` / `browser.newBrowserCDPSession()`,
// Puppeteer's `page.createCDPSession()`, and test-runner wrappers that name the
// thing they return (`ensureCDPHandler`) -- and a `send` on one of them takes a
// client-chosen method name (`Runtime.evaluate`, `Page.navigate`) that executes
// and writes with the browser's authority. A handler surface that both
// registers birpc functions and reaches one of these sessions is therefore a
// client-driven proxy over the whole debugging protocol, and the only thing
// that makes it safe is a permission gate the handler checks before proxying.
//
// The suppressing spellings are the ordinary readings of "is this caller
// allowed": a permission query (`checkPermission`, `isPermitted`,
// `hasPermission`), an explicit allow assertion (`assertCdpAllowed`,
// `isAllowed`), a config flag the surface honours (`allowWrite`, `allowExec`),
// an authorization or privilege query, and a role or admin gate. The list is
// matched generously on purpose: for a negation, matching too much costs
// recall rather than precision, and a registration that so much as names a
// permission gate is one whose gating has to be read by a human rather than
// guessed at here.
//
// The scope is the registering function rather than the individual handler,
// because a handler body's own context carries no fact about the `createBirpc`
// call that publishes it -- only the enclosing function sees both the
// registration and the privileged calls the registered bodies make. That is
// the same unit the Meteor and Express registrations are read at. Sibling
// handlers' own gates (`canWrite(project)` in the snapshot handlers) do not
// suppress: they answer whether *that* handler may write, not whether the CDP
// proxy is permitted, and reading them here would credit a gate the vulnerable
// handler never runs.
binding jsBirpcPrivilegedProtocolProxyWithoutPermissionGate {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "javascript" and containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["createBirpc"]) and containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["ensureCDPHandler", "newCDPSession", "createCDPSession", "newBrowserCDPSession"]) and not (containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["Allowed", "allowed", "Permission", "permission", "Permitted", "permitted", "Authorize", "authorize", "Authorized", "authorized", "Privilege", "privilege", "allowWrite", "allowExec", "AllowWrite", "AllowExec", "Role", "role", "Admin", "admin"]))
  emit issue code.SensitiveOperation at node
}
